 ## Microsoft Graph calendar
 
-Events live in the user's one calendar. Requests give times in any `timeZone` Graph takes, a Windows time zone's name or an IANA one's (CLDR maps the one to the other), UTC if none; an event keeps them as `originalStartTimeZone` and `originalEndTimeZone`. Responses give times in UTC, as with `Prefer: outlook.timezone="UTC"`; an all-day event's are its dates, wherever one is. A series' `recurrence` may follow a `daily`, `weekly`, `absoluteMonthly`, `absoluteYearly`, `relativeMonthly` or `relativeYearly` pattern, over an `endDate`, `numbered` or `noEnd` range (expanded three years ahead), its occurrences at the time of day its start has in its time zone, across daylight saving time. Changing or deleting one of its occurrences, by the occurrence's id, makes an exception of it. A `transactionId` given in creating an event is kept, and creating one with it again gives the event already created.
+Events live in the user's one calendar. Requests give times in any `timeZone` Graph takes, a Windows time zone's name or an IANA one's (CLDR maps the one to the other), UTC if none; an event keeps them as `originalStartTimeZone` and `originalEndTimeZone`. Responses give times in UTC, as with `Prefer: outlook.timezone="UTC"`, except that a POST or PATCH answers with the `start` and `end` it was given, in their zones, as Graph does; an all-day event's are its dates, wherever one is. A series' `recurrence` may follow a `daily`, `weekly`, `absoluteMonthly`, `absoluteYearly`, `relativeMonthly` or `relativeYearly` pattern, over an `endDate`, `numbered` or `noEnd` range (expanded three years ahead), its occurrences at the time of day its start has in its time zone, across daylight saving time. Changing or deleting one of its occurrences, by the occurrence's id, makes an exception of it. A `transactionId` given in creating an event is kept, and creating one with it again gives the event already created.
 
 An event keeps its `body` (`text` or `html`), `showAs`, `attendees` (each of a `type`, `required` unless given, and with a `status`, `none` unless given) and reminder (`isReminderOn`, `reminderMinutesBeforeStart`; 15 minutes by default) as given; `isOnlineMeeting: true` gives it an `onlineMeeting.joinUrl` at the emulator. What Graph sets itself of a meeting someone else organizes, the emulator takes too, to seed one: its `organizer`, the user's `responseStatus` (`notResponded` by default, `organizer` for the user's own) and `isCancelled`.
 
